Chapter: ProbeMemoryModel
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A memory probe small enough to hand-check. The subject we have been
 arguing with is the 2.5 MB x86-64 back end, where every quantity is
 entangled with every other and a wrong mechanism survives several
 experiments before anything contradicts it. Here the expected numbers
 can be worked out on paper, so a wrong mechanism fails on the first
 line that disagrees.

 There is one bump frontier. `__heap-save` reads it, `__heap-restore`
 rewinds it, `__heap-advance` reserves a span. Objects that must outlive
 a rewind go on the deck, a second cursor into the same heap that
 `__deck-enter` swaps into the allocation register while parking the
 frontier in the bivy. The contract section asserts relations between
 those cursors and must print byte-identically on every arm; it never
 prints an address, because the arms have different layouts and always
 will.

 `__deck-alloc` is absent below on purpose: the zig plug has no emitter
 for it and answers `@compileError`. The same claim is made by its
 decomposition -- enter, save, advance, save, exit -- which every arm
 does have, so the assertion survives the gap instead of being lost to
 it.

 The meters below the marker line are a different kind of output. They
 measure allocation as a `__heap-save` delta, which is only a measure of
 anything on an arm where objects come from the frontier. An arm that
 allocates elsewhere reports zero for all of them, and that zero is the
 answer to a real question rather than a broken probe, so the meter
 section names the condition first and the detector says UNMEASURED
 rather than dividing by it.

Section: Instrument

 `deck-record` is a name the code generator intercepts, bracketing its
 argument's evaluation in a deck extent. The stub is what the type
 checker reads and what an arm without the interception falls back to.

  deck-record : a -> a
  deck-record (x) = x

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

 The probe is a lambda so the closure is built at the call site, before
 the window opens, and its cost cannot land inside the measurement; the
 result is threaded through the guard so nothing can drop the work the
 window is there to observe. The rewind makes each measurement
 independent of every other, which is what lets a quadratic reading be
 taken without the run growing to match. Only the Integer escapes the
 window, so nothing reclaimed is read.

  measure : (Integer -> Integer) -> Integer
  measure (f) =
   let h0 = __heap-save
   in let r = f 0
   in let h1 = __heap-save
   in let back = __heap-restore h0
   in if r >= 0 then h1 - h0 else 0 - 1

  ratio-text : Integer, Integer -> Text
  ratio-text (prev) (cur) =
   if prev <= 0 then "n/a"
   else let t = (cur * 10 + prev / 2) / prev
   in integer-to-text (t / 10) & "." & integer-to-text (t - (t / 10) * 10)

 Doubling n doubles a linear cost and quadruples a quadratic one, so the
 two ratios separate at 3.0 with a wide margin either side. Both ratios
 must agree; one of each is a shape neither name fits and saying so is
 better than picking the nearer lie.

  verdict : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  verdict (a) (b) (c) =
   if a <= 0 then "UNMEASURED"
   else let r1 = (b * 10 + a / 2) / a
   in let r2 = (c * 10 + b / 2) / b
   in if (r1 >= 30) & (r2 >= 30) then "QUADRATIC"
   else if (r1 < 30) & (r2 < 30) then "LINEAR"
   else "MIXED"

Section: Shapes

  cap-list : Integer -> List Integer
  cap-list (n) = __list-with-capacity n

  push-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  push-loop (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else push-loop (i + 1) n (list-push acc i)

  cat-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cat-loop (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else cat-loop (i + 1) n (acc & [i])

  text-loop : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  text-loop (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else text-loop (i + 1) n (acc & "x")

Section: Contract

 The mark is eight megabytes above the frontier: far enough that no
 allocation made under one cursor can be mistaken for one made under the
 other, and far enough that the largest reading the detector takes -- a
 quarter megabyte, rewound as soon as it is read -- cannot climb into
 the deck and overwrite what the deck is holding.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let bivy = __heap-save
   in let mark = bivy + 8388608
   in let m = __deck-set mark
   in let e1 = __deck-enter
   in let on-deck = __heap-save
   in let d1 = __deck-exit
   in let off-deck = __heap-save
   in let n1 = __deck-enter
   in let n2 = __deck-enter
   in let n3 = __deck-exit
   in let still-on-deck = __heap-save
   in let n4 = __deck-exit
   in let after-nest = __heap-save
   in let r1 = __deck-enter
   in let res0 = __heap-save
   in let r2 = __heap-advance 64
   in let res1 = __heap-save
   in let r3 = __deck-exit
   in let trip0 = __heap-save
   in let t1 = __heap-advance 4096
   in let t2 = __heap-restore trip0
   in let trip1 = __heap-save
   in let keep0 = __heap-save
   in let kept = deck-record (cat-loop 0 8 [])
   in let k1 = __heap-restore keep0
   in let survived = list-length kept
   in let metered = measure (\z -> __heap-advance (z + 1024))
   in let observes = measure (\z -> list-length [z, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])
   in act
     print-line-uni ("enter switches to the mark : " & show-yn (on-deck == mark))
     print-line-uni ("outer exit restores bivy   : " & show-yn (off-deck == bivy))
     print-line-uni ("inner exit does not restore: " & show-yn (still-on-deck == mark))
     print-line-uni ("outer exit does restore    : " & show-yn (after-nest == bivy))
     print-line-uni ("mark and bivy are distinct : " & show-yn (mark > bivy))
     print-line-uni ("deck reserve bumps by size : " & show-yn (res1 - res0 == 64))
     print-line-uni ("save/restore round trips   : " & show-yn (trip1 == trip0))
     print-line-uni ("deck-record survives rewind: " & show-yn (survived == 8))
     print-line-uni ("meter reads a 1024 reserve : " & show-yn (metered == 1024))
     print-line-uni ("ratio arithmetic 2x and 4x : " & show-yn (ratio-text 100 201 == "2.0"))
     print-line-uni ("verdict names the two shapes: " & show-yn ((verdict 100 201 402 == "LINEAR") & (verdict 100 405 1620 == "QUADRATIC")))

     print-line-uni "-- meters below this line are expected to differ between arms --"
     print-line-uni ("frontier observes allocation: " & show-yn (observes > 0))
     print-line-uni ("list literal 1  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length [z])))
     print-line-uni ("list literal 2  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length [z, 1])))
     print-line-uni ("list literal 4  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length [z, 1, 2, 3])))
     print-line-uni ("list literal 8  " & integer-to-text observes)
     print-line-uni ("concat 4 & 4    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length ([z, 1, 2, 3] & [4, 5, 6, 7]))))
     print-line-uni ("chain a&b&c&d   " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length ([z] & [1] & [2] & [3]))))
     print-line-uni ("push loop 16    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length (push-loop z 16 []))))
     print-line-uni ("capacity 0      " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length (cap-list z))))
     print-line-uni ("capacity 16     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length (cap-list (z + 16)))))
     print-line-uni ("capacity 256    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length (cap-list (z + 256)))))
     print-line-uni ("text concat 2   " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> text-length ("abcd" & "efgh"))))
     print-line-uni ("List Integer 4  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length [z, 1, 2, 3])))
     print-line-uni ("List Text 4     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> list-length ["a", "b", "c", "d"])))

     print-line-uni "-- accumulator shapes: n, 2n, 4n and the ratios --"
     let c1 = measure (\z -> list-length (cat-loop z 64 []))
     in let c2 = measure (\z -> list-length (cat-loop z 128 []))
     in let c3 = measure (\z -> list-length (cat-loop z 256 []))
     in let p1 = measure (\z -> list-length (push-loop z 64 []))
     in let p2 = measure (\z -> list-length (push-loop z 128 []))
     in let p3 = measure (\z -> list-length (push-loop z 256 []))
     in let x1 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop z 64 ""))
     in let x2 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop z 128 ""))
     in let x3 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop z 256 ""))
     in act
       print-line-uni ("cat-accum  " & integer-to-text c1 & " " & integer-to-text c2 & " " & integer-to-text c3
        & "  ratios " & ratio-text c1 c2 & " " & ratio-text c2 c3 & " -> " & verdict c1 c2 c3)
       print-line-uni ("push-accum " & integer-to-text p1 & " " & integer-to-text p2 & " " & integer-to-text p3
        & "  ratios " & ratio-text p1 p2 & " " & ratio-text p2 p3 & " -> " & verdict p1 p2 p3)
       print-line-uni ("text-accum " & integer-to-text x1 & " " & integer-to-text x2 & " " & integer-to-text x3
        & "  ratios " & ratio-text x1 x2 & " " & ratio-text x2 x3 & " -> " & verdict x1 x2 x3)
     end
   end
  end
